 "script": "How are Barcelona scoring so many goals this season without using a natural striker? This summer, Barcelona lost every striker on their roster, because Robert Lewandowski left for free and Marcus Rashford's loan ended, and those two alone took thirty-one goals with them. Their other forward, Ferran Torres, left for PSG as well, which left coach Hansi Flick with zero players who actually play as a natural number nine. But his first move to fix it ran straight into a wall he couldn't get past. First, he tried the normal way by chasing Atlético Madrid's Julián Alvarez, but Atlético shut the door instantly and refused to sell even for two hundred million euros. So Flick stopped hunting for a striker and built an attack without one, and in Barcelona's opening game against Elche, he shoved his captain Raphinha into the middle as a fake striker. Raphinha scored twice and set up another goal, while Fermín López, a midfielder, came flying into the box and grabbed two more himself. But the reason this keeps working has nothing to do with luck, and if you're enjoying the video, please hit the follow button. Barcelona won five to nothing with only seven shots on target while creating nine gigantic chances. Flick keeps his forwards constantly swapping positions while his midfielders sprint in from deep, so defenders never know who to grab, and then his players press high and steal the ball right next to the goal. That means Barcelona keep attacking a defense that's already scrambled and out of shape, which is exactly how they score this many goals without ever needing a real striker.",
